033 2231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049092 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "d9ba8adc-063a-54f1-8d4e-3ac01a6c2237" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1031 "Understanding Proportional Relationships From Descriptions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1033 2554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049141 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "de04f42d-8248-5b77-8683-487f694fba77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186138430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186138431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186138432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1031 "Modeling with Direct Variation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1033 2274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049281 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "e9994744-cb83-57d9-8154-ea5ac4c05cc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135494 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135494 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135494 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135494 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186498828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135494 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "458ebd28-0d5b-5e34-ac8f-0865001d94d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138166 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14086" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138166 #edb/ref 1071 "The force $F,$ measured in $\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2$, applied to a motorcycle varies directly with its acceleration $a,$ measured in $\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}^2.$ When the acceleration of the motorcycle is $a=5\\,\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}^2,$ the force is $F=2\\,000\\,\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2.$ What is the constant of variation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138166 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f01f91de-9010-5429-ba83-faaf90c866d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138166 #edb/ref 1074 "The force $F$ is directly proportional to the acceleration $a,$ so we have\n\n$$\nF = k{a},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the acceleration is $a=5\\, \\textrm{m}/\\,\\textrm{s}^2,$ the force is $F=2\\,000\\,\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,000 & =k\\cdot (5) \\\\ 2\\,000 & =5k \\\\ \\frac{2\\,000}{5} & =k \\\\ 400 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k = \\dfrac{F}{a},$ we have that the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{kg m/s}}^{2}}{\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cancel{{\\text{m/s}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\text{kg}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k = 400\\,\\textrm{kg}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138168 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138168 #edb/ref 1071 "The stretch $s,$ measured in centimeters $(\\textrm{cm}),$ of a loaded spring varies directly with the load $l,$ measured in grams $(\\mathrm{g}).$ When the load is $l=8\\,\\mathrm{g},$ the stretch is $s=9.6\\,\\mathrm{cm}.$ Find the load when the stretch is $s=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138168 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1408bad-ae5c-55fb-b78e-60ee0d8a1ca0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138168 #edb/ref 1074 "The stretch $s$ is directly proportional to the load $l,$ so we have\n\n$$\ns = k{l},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when $l=8\\,\\mathrm{g},$ we have $s=9.6\\,\\mathrm{cm}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9.6 & =k\\cdot (8) \\\\ 9.6 & =8k \\\\ \\frac{9.6}{8} & =k \\\\ 1.2 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\ns = 1.2l.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the load when the stretch is $s=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l & =\\frac{s}{1.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7.2}{1.2} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the load when $s=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ is $l=6 \\,\\textrm{g}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138171 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14091" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138171 #edb/ref 1071 "The force $F$, in newtons ($\\mathrm N$), that an elastic spring exerts on an object varies directly with the **square** of the extension of the spring $L,$ measured in meters. If the force is $F = 4\\,\\mathrm N$ when $L=0.1\\,\\mathrm m,$ then what equation models this situation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138171 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "201cba9a-1d9e-5bf3-b312-d84fb3d59407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138171 #edb/ref 1074 "The force $F$ is directly proportional to the square of the spring extension $L$, so we have\n\n$$\nF = kL^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $F=4\\,\\textrm{N}$ when $L=0.1\\,\\textrm m.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =k\\cdot (0.1)^{2} \\\\ 4 & =k\\cdot 0.01 \\\\ \\frac{4}{0.01} & =k \\\\ 400 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nF = 400L^2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138430 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138430 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138430 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138430 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138430 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138430 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138430 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Constant of Variation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138430 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138430 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a35a2fee-a1a5-59aa-9390-f73d2898320c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138431 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138431 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138431 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138431 #edb/ref 1039 "Modeling With Direct Variation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138431 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138431 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7df32abc-07bd-5213-a838-50e893ff5759" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138432 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138432 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138432 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138432 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138432 #edb/ref 1039 "Modeling Direct Variations With Second Powers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138432 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138432 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "01db3ebe-66ec-5340-8e29-c99fb149f5da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138600 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138600 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138600 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138600 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138600 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138600 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138600 #edb/ref 1045 "Modeling with Direct Variation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138600 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186139353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138600 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138600 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138600 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "753f3c2c-963f-5bf9-82db-0290639a9a4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138738 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186135494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138738 #edb/ref 1069 "q-210541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138738 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138738 #edb/ref 1071 "The volume of space $V$ a particular gas occupies, measured in liters $(L),$ is directly proportional to its temperature $T,$ measured in Kelvin $(\\text{K}).$ The volume of the gas is $V=48\\,\\text{L}$ when its temperature is $T=360\\,\\text{K}.$ Find the volume of the gas when its temperature is $300\\,\\text{K}.$\n\nThe volume of the gas is\n{{field-1}}$\\text{L}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138738 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "97912ab9-bc49-5f4d-855e-74d3a88b6f09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138738 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138738 #edb/ref 1074 "The volume of the gas can be modeled as a direct variation\n\n$$\nV=kT,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that the gas occupies $V=48\\,\\text{L}$ at $T=360\\,\\text{K}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & =k\\cdot 360 \\\\ \\frac{48}{360} & =k \\\\ \\frac{2}{15} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{2}{15}T.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the volume occupied by the gas when $T=300\\,\\text{K}$:\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot 300=40\n$$\n\nTherefore, when the temperature of the gas is $300\\,\\text{K},$ its volume is $40\\,\\text{L}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139353 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139353 #edb/ref 1061 196782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139353 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "577c6468-9f0e-55fb-84fc-17fcc4e6bca8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139353 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139354 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139354 #edb/ref 1061 196783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139354 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd7ad040-afed-5ba6-ad72-f3ed0909fc54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139354 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139355 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139355 #edb/ref 1061 196786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139355 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d2efc257-0a27-570d-a356-98d12f63e6c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139355 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139356 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139356 #edb/ref 1061 196789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139356 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "80f09102-b1f8-5a4e-8df1-f285f0944581" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139356 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139357 #edb/ref 1061 196790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139357 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "458a0b34-716e-5233-8ee6-52e6a9afd6a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139357 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139717 #edb/ref 1064 "A good example of a direct variation model is the distance an object travels when moving at a constant rate. In this case, the corresponding direct variation equation is \n\n$$\nd=vt,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $d$ is the total distance traveled by the object,\n\n- $t$ is the total time that the object has been traveling, and,\n\n- $v$ is the constant of proportionality, called the **speed** of the object.\n\nFor instance, if an object travels a distance of $24\\,\\textrm m$ in $4$ seconds, then we can substitute $d=24\\,\\textrm m$ and $t=4\\,\\textrm s$ into the above equation and solve for $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =v\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{24}{4} & =v \\\\ 6 & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation that relates $d$ and $t$ is\n\n$$\nd=6t.\n$$\n\n \n\nOne thing to note is that the constant of proportionality $v = 6$ also has units. So let's work out what these units are.\n\nSince we have\n\n$$\nd = vt,\n$$\n\nit follows that\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{d}{t}.\n$$\n\nSo, the units of $v$ are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm m}{\\textrm s} = \\textrm m / \\textrm s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality (including its unit) is $v = 6\\, \\textrm m / \\textrm s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139717 #edb/ref 1065 8753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139717 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dcda3158-97b6-5a4a-b062-3803a3db7b26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139717 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139719 #edb/ref 1064 "When modeling proportional relationships, we sometimes find that a quantity $y$ varies directly with the *square* of a second quantity $x.$ In such cases, we write\n\n$$\ny = kx^2.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider a circle of radius $r,$ measured in centimeters.\n\n![](images/fa/fad3151a754d96d3e4ad97fa812ce7087601cebe7c4ab00f7b3c8cd603b140c0.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the area $A,$ measured in square meters, of a circle varies directly with the *square* of the radius $r.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\nA = k r^2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139719 #edb/ref 1065 8755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139719 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c5a8d87f-edfb-5e37-ad6d-6cafde48e4ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139719 #edb/ref 1067 "Variations With Second Powers" #edb/ref 17592186045417