592186045417] [17592186499392 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a8b9ec7b-af39-5a0f-ac6b-c6ef3096bc15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499430 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499430 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499430 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499430 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f89aa7c6-1fef-528f-abe7-c21638260283" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499473 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499473 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116487" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499473 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499473 #edb/ref 1071 "The fuel cost $C,$ in dollars per hour, for running a train is directly proportional to the **square** of its speed $v,$ measured in miles per hour. If it costs $$15$ per hour to run a train that moves at a speed of $100$ miles per hour, what equation models this situation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499473 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58ce1cea-1782-532b-bc46-409e39fb6f1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499473 #edb/ref 1074 "The fuel cost $C$ is directly proportional to the square of the speed $v,$ so we have\n\n$$\nC=k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $C=15$ dollars per hour when $v=100\\,\\text{mph}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =k\\cdot {100}^{2} \\\\ 15 & =k\\cdot 10\\,000 \\\\ \\frac{15}{10\\,000} & =k \\\\ 0.0015 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nC=0.0015{v}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499522 #edb/ref 1069 "q-236468" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499522 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499522 #edb/ref 1071 "The force $F,$ measured in newtons $(\\text{N})$, needed to extend a spring by some length $l,$ measured in $\\text{m},$ is directly proportional to $l.$ When the extension of the spring is $l=0.005\\,\\text{m},$ the force on the spring is $F=25\\,\\text{N}.$ What is the constant of variation?\n\nThe constant of variation is\n{{field-1}}$\\text{N/m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499522 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ab23edc2-70be-5457-b5af-37bf9a82da09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499522 #edb/ref 1074 "The force $F$ is directly proportional to the extension $l,$ so we have\n\n$$\nF=kl,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the extension is $l=0.005\\,\\text{m},$ the force is $F=25\\,\\text{N}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25 & =k\\cdot (0.005) \\\\ 25 & =0.005k \\\\ \\frac{25}{0.005} & =k \\\\ 5\\,000 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=\\frac{F}{l},$ we have that the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{N}}{\\text{m}} & =\\text{N/m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k=5\\,000\\,\\text{N/m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499532 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499532 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116482" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499532 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499532 #edb/ref 1071 "At a car rental agency, the charge $C,$ in dollars, for renting a car varies directly with the **square** of the distance  $d$ traveled by the car, measured in miles. If it costs $$5$ to rent a car to drive $20$ miles, then what equation models this situation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499532 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bcc34549-4b02-5866-8337-252ca34764e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499532 #edb/ref 1074 "The charge $C$ is directly proportional to the square of the distance $d,$ so we have\n\n$$\nC=k{d}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $C=$5$ when $d=20\\,\\text{mi}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =k\\cdot {20}^{2} \\\\ 5 & =k\\cdot 400 \\\\ \\frac{5}{400} & =k \\\\ \\frac{1}{80} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{80}{d}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499543 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499543 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499543 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499543 #edb/ref 1071 "The price $P,$ in dollars, of electricity in a certain city is directly proportional to the consumption $C,$ measured in kilowatt-hour ($\\text{kWh}).$ When the consumption is $C=24\\,\\text{kWh},$ the price is $P=$264.$ What is the constant of variation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4a7e4f5-44ad-5538-a987-14a969b63b6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499543 #edb/ref 1074 "The price $P$ is directly proportional to the consumption $C,$ so we have\n\n$$\nP=kC,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the consumption is $C=24\\,\\text{kWh},$ the price is $P=$264.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}264 & =k\\cdot (24) \\\\ 264 & =24k \\\\ \\frac{264}{24} & =k \\\\ 11 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=\\frac{P}{C},$ we have that the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{dollars}}{\\text{kWh}} & =\\text{dollars per kilowatt-hour}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k=$11$ per kilowatt-hour." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499547 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499547 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116379" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499547 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499547 #edb/ref 1071 "The amount of bleach $b,$ measured in gallons ($gal$), in a certain detergent product varies directly with the amount of water $w,$ measured in gallons, in the product. When the amount of bleach is $b=1.25\\,gal,$ the amount of water is $w=3.75\\,gal.$ Find the amount of bleach when the amount of water is $w=9\\,gal.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499547 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db05b6d0-7f2f-569f-a84a-34026d2fb3c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499547 #edb/ref 1074 "The amount of bleach $b$ is directly proportional to the amount of water $w,$ so we have\n\n$$\nb=kw.\n$$\n\nWe are told that when the amount of bleach is $b=1.25\\,gal,$ the amount of water is $w=3.75\\,gal.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.25 & =k\\cdot (3.75) \\\\ 1.25 & =3.75k \\\\ \\frac{1.25}{3.75} & =k \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}w.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the amount of bleach when the amount of water is  $w=9\\,gal,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (9)=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amount of bleach when $w=9\\,gal$ is $b=3\\,\\text{gal}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
